# The SDK is MIT or Apache-2.0 and free, so the live SDK Commercial Terms page
# is gone. Its addresses go to the licensing page. The executed dated copies
# under /legal/terms/ stay published, so these rules are exact and no splat
# covers /legal/terms/.
/terms /licensing/ 308
/terms/ /licensing/ 308
/legal/terms /licensing/ 308
/legal/terms/ /licensing/ 308
/terms/2026-07-23 /legal/terms/2026-07-23/ 308
/terms/2026-07-23/ /legal/terms/2026-07-23/ 308
/terms/2026-09-10 /legal/terms/2026-09-10/ 308
/terms/2026-09-10/ /legal/terms/2026-09-10/ 308
/privacy /legal/privacy/ 308
/privacy/ /legal/privacy/ 308
/terms/* /legal/terms/:splat 308
# /evaluate was the security review pack. It folded into /security, which now
# carries every claim it made. The sitemap had already published /evaluate/ to
# crawlers, so the route has real inbound links even pre-launch.
/evaluate /security/ 308
/evaluate/ /security/ 308
# /demo was the scenario page. It folded into the homepage, and the section it
# folded into — four scenarios that ran the SDK into a failure on purpose — was
# cut from that page later. What survives under this name is the live demo, so
# the target is the exhibit inside `DemoConsole.astro`: the fragment is on the
# animation rather than on the paragraph above it, which is argued where the id
# is written.
#
# The target carries a fragment because Cloudflare's redirects support them in
# the destination ("Can include fragments, query strings, splats, and
# placeholders"), and only a line-leading # is a comment. The sitemap published
# /demo/ too.
#
# The cost, recorded because it is a real one: a destination fragment replaces
# whatever fragment the reader arrived with, so /demo#flip-a-byte lands on the
# demo rather than on a scenario. It would now land nowhere in any case — the
# scenario ids left with the section — and bare /demo is what the sitemap, the
# old nav item, and any bookmark all point at.
/demo /#demo 308
/demo/ /#demo 308
# /compare was the comparison page. Its table and its five project notes are now
# the two bands under "How it compares" on /product, so the destination carries
# that section's id.
#
# The fragment costs less here than it did for /demo: /compare published no deep
# links of its own — it had no ids on any of its four sections — so nothing that
# arrives with a fragment can have it replaced. The sitemap had already given
# /compare/ to crawlers, and it was a nav item on every page of the site, which
# is the widest inbound surface any route here has had.
/compare /product/#how-it-compares 308
/compare/ /product/#how-it-compares 308
# /learn was the architecture guide. It made the same argument as the blog post
# it now points at, down to a title collision — "How E2EE changes your
# architecture" against "E2EE changes your application architecture" — and the
# post carries a source for each claim where the page asserted them bare.
#
# No fragment: /learn's seven sections had ids, but the post's headings do not
# match them one for one, so a wrong anchor would be worse than the top of the
# article. The sitemap published /learn/ too, and two pages linked it inline.
/learn /blog/e2ee-changes-your-application-architecture/ 308
/learn/ /blog/e2ee-changes-your-application-architecture/ 308
# /relay/pricing was the Relay limits page: the plan table, the meters, and the
# overage rates. All of it now lives on /pricing, where the plans it priced
# already were, so the destination carries the plan table's id. The sitemap
# published /relay/pricing/, the Relay service terms cite it by name, and the
# console docs linked it.
/relay/pricing /pricing/#relay-plans 308
/relay/pricing/ /pricing/#relay-plans 308
# /legal/relay-beta-limits was the live Relay limits document while Relay was a
# beta. Relay is generally available, and the document is now the Service
# Limits at /legal/signal-relay-service-limits. Every frozen Relay terms version
# up to relay-2026-09-29 links the old address, and those pages are never
# edited, so the rule keeps their link working. It points at the current
# address directly, so the old link takes one hop. The rules are exact: the
# dated copies under /legal/relay-beta-limits/<date> are permanent addresses
# and still serve.
/legal/relay-beta-limits /legal/signal-relay-service-limits/ 308
/legal/relay-beta-limits/ /legal/signal-relay-service-limits/ 308
# The live Relay terms, retention statement, and service limits moved to the
# signal-relay addresses that name the Signal Protocol Relay. Accepted terms,
# DPA, and privacy versions link the old live addresses, and those pages are
# never edited, so these rules are permanent. The rules are exact: the dated
# copies under /legal/relay-terms/<date>, /legal/relay-retention/<date>, and
# /legal/relay-service-limits/<date> are permanent addresses and still serve.
/legal/relay-terms /legal/signal-relay-terms/ 308
/legal/relay-terms/ /legal/signal-relay-terms/ 308
/legal/relay-retention /legal/signal-relay-retention/ 308
/legal/relay-retention/ /legal/signal-relay-retention/ 308
/legal/relay-service-limits /legal/signal-relay-service-limits/ 308
/legal/relay-service-limits/ /legal/signal-relay-service-limits/ 308
